>
> White men can't jump.
> lo kapli nakni cu na kakne lo nu plipe
>
This one looks good, though in modern usage {kakne} is usually used with=20
{ka} instead of {nu}: {lo kapli nakni cu na kakne lo ka [ce'u] plipe}.=20

> lo kapli nakni na ka'e plipe=20
>

> I can't jump; possibly because I am a white man.
> mi na kakne lo nu plipe ki'u da'i mi lo kapli nakni
>
First, {ki'u} takes one sumti (an abstraction), so the clause should be=20
{ki'u da'i lo nu mi kapli nakni}.
*mi na kakne lo ka plipe ki'u da'i lo nu mi kapli nakni*
However, note that {nu} abstractions encapsulate an entire bridi, so to get=
=20
the {ki'u} outside of the abstraction, where it belongs, you need to=20
terminate the abstraction with {kei} (or {ku}, or {vau}).
*mi na kakne lo ka plipe kei ki'u da'i lo nu mi kapli nakni*
However (again), that leaves a subtle problem with scope. {na} scopes over=
=20
the {ki'u} clause, so this says "It's not true that (I can jump because I'm=
=20
a white man)", i.e. "Being a white man is not the reason that I can jump".=
=20
It should be flipped to get the correct scoping, as in {ki'u da'i lo nu mi=
=20
kapli nakni kei mi na kakne lo ka plipe}, or you can split it into two=20
jufra as {mi na kakne lo ka plipe .i da'i lo nu mi kapli nakni cu krinu [lo=
=20
du'u go'i]}.
(Sorry. Negation can make things complicated.)

>
> Are these translations correct?
> xu le xe fanva drani=20
>
> In the second sentence I want to indicate that I don't actually know why =
I=20
> am unable to jump;=20
> however, it could be because I'm a white man.
>
> ki'e
>

je'e mu'o mi'e la durkavore
=20

